      "writtenOn": "2021-08-16T20:17:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m okay with this, Dai, do you have any safety concerns? What\u0027s the \"worst\" that could happen if someone misuses one of those v4p1 Brian?",
      "revId": "90f31bba1bc939951ad9c1de17f678e2c23120f5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e7bd483f_95e755e5",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347380
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-16T20:55:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This had previously been tested with the 3A cables and earlier Servo_v4p1\u0027s that used the smaller shunt resistors under a continuous 90W load. That\u0027s a worst case situation combining both a sustained high load and the lowest rated components. There was no indication of overheating within the Servo_V4p1.\n\nThis has also already been deployed to ToT so most of the Servo_V4p1 images have already been operating with this change. The change will also have no impact on DUTs that request the lower 60W and 3A current limits.",
      "parentUuid": "50f823da_a1eb4009",
      "revId": "90f31bba1bc939951ad9c1de17f678e2c23120f5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e15b2055_6e15930c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-23T20:58:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Can we detect \"servo_v4p1 devices with 3A cables\" by a board revision or something?",
      "parentUuid": "e7bd483f_95e755e5",
      "revId": "90f31bba1bc939951ad9c1de17f678e2c23120f5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "85ce59fe_54462285",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347380
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-23T21:30:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "These were the early development units and very few are even working still. We\u0027d have to have one of the few remaining early Servo_V4p1 devices combined with both a charger that supports 5A and a DUT that requests it. Chromestop doesn\u0027t supply those chargers by default and only a few DUTs request 5A. It\u0027s a very rare combination.\n\nThat rare combination was tested b:176110980 and showed it safe on the older servo hardware. Adding version control logic isn\u0027t needed to safely operate these devices since the elevated temperature was mild. It\u0027s only adding 0.125W of heat into the system at the full 5A.",
      "parentUuid": "e15b2055_6e15930c",
      "revId": "90f31bba1bc939951ad9c1de17f678e2c23120f5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2e4ee4ab_22047462",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-23T22:31:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m convinced it\u0027s safe. Those 3A servos may not be able to supply a requested voltage to a DUT because of the extra R from the 3A cable. (Do you have any measurements on this?) So, I\u0027m wondering if we can provide a warning when we detect them or simply make servod refuse to connect and print \"your servo is not supported\".",
      "parentUuid": "85ce59fe_54462285",
      "revId": "90f31bba1bc939951ad9c1de17f678e2c23120f5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "163b259f_0d80fcb4",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1347380
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-24T00:15:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was measuring a total circuit impedance of 0.120 ohms on the 5A cables so that would translate into 0.6V. I just ran a test with a 0.5A load and a 3A servo_v4p1. The voltage drop seen is about 0.065V. This would be a 0.65V drop at 5A and servo adds 0.130 ohms of resistance with the 3A cables compared to 0.120 ohms with the 5A cables. It looks like it\u0027s pretty close to the maximum impedance.\n\nIt\u0027s pretty close to the Figure 4-1 max IR drops:\nhttps://www.usb.org/sites/default/files/USB%20Type-C%20Spec%20R2.0%20-%20August%202019.pdf",
      "parentUuid": "2e4ee4ab_22047462",
      "revId": "90f31bba1bc939951ad9c1de17f678e2c23120f5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
